        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The sigmoidoscope provides visual access to the distal large bowel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Flexible type:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The lubricated endoscope is introduced through the anus.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The operator advances it carefully under direct video visualisation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The tip is manoeuvred through the rectum and, where appropriate, into the sigmoid colon.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The mucosa is inspected systematically, with suction or irrigation used when necessary.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Biopsies or selected interventions may be performed through the instrument channel.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The scope is withdrawn carefully, with continued inspection of the mucosa.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rigid type: The lubricated tube is inserted according to the specific instrument's technique, with an obturator where provided. The obturator is removed when appropriate, and the lumen is examined under illumination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The procedure must be performed by appropriately trained personnel. The scope should never be advanced forcefully against resistance or severe pain."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Types and comparison",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Flexible sigmoidoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Flexible insertion tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Camera and monitor-based visualisation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can navigate the curves of the rectum and sigmoid colon.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Usually permits biopsy and suction through dedicated channels.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires specialised equipment and reprocessing.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Rigid sigmoidoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Straight, rigid barrel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Often used for direct examination of the rectum and selected distal bowel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be simpler in design.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Has limited ability to follow bowel curvature and is not equivalent to flexible sigmoidoscopy in reach.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Indication",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Rectal bleeding",
                      "Identify possible distal bowel sources."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected colorectal polyps",
                      "Visualise lesions and obtain tissue samples when indicated."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected rectal or sigmoid tumour",
                      "Identify suspicious lesions for further investigation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inflammatory bowel disease",
                      "Assess distal colonic inflammation and obtain biopsies."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Proctitis",
                      "Examine inflammation of the rectum."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Altered bowel habits",
                      "Investigate selected symptoms when distal bowel assessment is appropriate."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Follow-up",
                      "Assess selected known distal bowel conditions."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Biopsy",
                      "Obtain tissue for histopathological examination."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A flexible sigmoidoscopy may be used for selected screening or surveillance pathways, depending on local guidelines and the patient's risk profile."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: A normal sigmoidoscopy does not exclude disease in the proximal colon. Colonoscopy or other investigations may be necessary if symptoms, risk factors or findings warrant further evaluation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Direct visualisation of the rectum and sigmoid colon.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows targeted biopsy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Flexible systems can navigate bowel curvature.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Often requires less extensive preparation than full colonoscopy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Frequently performed without sedation, although this depends on the patient and clinical setting.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can identify distal bowel inflammation, polyps and suspicious masses.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Does not examine the entire colon.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bowel preparation may still be required.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause abdominal discomfort, bloating or cramping.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Flexible systems are expensive and require specialist equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inadequate preparation can obscure important lesions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A detected lesion may require a subsequent colonoscopy for full assessment or treatment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rigid instruments have more limited reach and flexibility.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The decision to perform sigmoidoscopy depends on the clinical indication, the patient's stability and the anticipated risks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Important situations requiring deferral, specialist assessment or additional precautions include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected bowel perforation or peritonitis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Haemodynamic instability or shock.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Severe acute colitis, particularly when perforation risk is elevated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Severe anorectal pain or stenosis that prevents safe passage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Recent colorectal surgery, depending on the procedure and healing status.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Significant bleeding or suspected obstruction requiring urgent assessment and a tailored investigation plan.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anticoagulant use, bleeding disorders and significant comorbidities require individual assessment, especially if biopsy or therapeutic intervention is planned. They are not automatic contraindications to every diagnostic examination."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Procedure: OSCE approach",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Preparation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the procedure, obtain informed consent, review relevant history and medications, assess allergies and bleeding risk, and ensure the prescribed bowel preparation has been completed. Arrange privacy and a chaperone according to local policy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Positioning"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The left lateral position is commonly used, with the hips and knees flexed. Confirm the patient's comfort and monitor vital signs as clinically indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Preliminary examination"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the perianal region and perform a digital rectal examination when appropriate. Assess for tenderness, masses, stenosis or other findings that may affect safe insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Insertion and advancement"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Introduce the scope gently under direct visualisation. The trained operator advances it carefully, using appropriate manoeuvres to navigate bowel curvature. Stop if significant resistance, severe pain or concerning findings occur."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Systematic inspection"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the mucosa for inflammation, ulceration, polyps, masses, bleeding and vascular abnormalities. Use suction or irrigation as needed. Take biopsies when indicated and within the operator's competence."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/sigmoidoscope_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 6 — Completion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Withdraw carefully, assess the patient's condition, document the extent and quality of examination, record findings and samples, and arrange follow-up. Reprocess reusable equipment according to the manufacturer's instructions and validated infection-prevention protocols."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Normal and abnormal findings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Possible significance"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Smooth, intact pink mucosa",
                      "Normal appearance, depending on the bowel segment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Erythema and friability",
                      "Inflammatory disease or proctitis."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ulcers",
                      "Inflammatory, infectious, ischaemic or other pathology."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Polyp",
                      "Benign or potentially precancerous lesion; histology may be needed."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Irregular or ulcerated mass",
                      "Suspicious for malignancy; requires further investigation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Diverticular openings",
                      "Diverticulosis, if present in the examined segment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fresh blood",
                      "Active or recent bleeding requiring assessment of its source."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Faecal residue obscuring mucosa",
                      "Limited examination; relevant pathology may have been missed."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A lesion's appearance alone does not reliably establish its histology. Biopsy and appropriate follow-up are important when indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Abdominal discomfort and bloating",
                      "May result from bowel distension and scope manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Minor bleeding",
                      "Can occur, particularly after biopsy."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vasovagal reaction",
                      "May cause sweating, nausea, bradycardia or hypotension."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mucosal injury",
                      "May result from mechanical trauma."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bowel perforation",
                      "Rare but serious; risk is influenced by bowel pathology and the procedure."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Uncommon with appropriate infection-prevention measures."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Missed lesion",
                      "Can occur if preparation is inadequate or the examination is incomplete."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe or worsening abdominal pain, fever, heavy bleeding, fainting or abdominal distension after the procedure requires urgent medical assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "sigmoidoscope_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "sigmoidoscope_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A sigmoidoscope, used to examine the rectum and sigmoid colon."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the difference between a sigmoidoscope and a colonoscope?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A sigmoidoscope examines the rectum and sigmoid colon, whereas a colonoscope is designed to examine the colon more extensively, usually to the caecum when the examination is complete."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between rigid and flexible sigmoidoscopy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rigid sigmoidoscopy uses a straight tube with limited reach. Flexible sigmoidoscopy uses a flexible camera-equipped endoscope that can navigate bowel curvature and generally examine more of the distal colon."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the function of the instrument channel?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It permits the passage of compatible instruments, such as biopsy forceps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why is bowel preparation important?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It removes faecal material that could obscure the mucosa, improving visualisation and reducing the chance of missing lesions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Name four indications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rectal bleeding, suspected distal colorectal polyps, suspected rectal or sigmoid tumours, and assessment of distal inflammatory bowel disease."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Name two serious complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bowel perforation and significant haemorrhage, particularly in association with interventions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Can a normal sigmoidoscopy exclude colorectal cancer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It cannot exclude lesions in unexamined portions of the colon, and further investigation may be needed based on symptoms and risk factors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What is the difference between an anoscope, proctoscope and sigmoidoscope?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An anoscope primarily examines the anal canal; a proctoscope examines the anal canal and distal rectum; a sigmoidoscope examines the rectum and sigmoid colon."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
